# 4. Shared diagram canvas engine

The canvas is the central technical and experiential foundation.

Every specialized editor will use the same interaction infrastructure while extending it with diagram-specific behavior.

## 4.1 Object model

Every canvas element must support a consistent base schema.

Common properties:

- Unique identifier
- Object type
- Position
- Dimensions
- Rotation
- Scale
- Layer
- Z-index
- Visibility
- Lock state
- Style
- Custom metadata
- Connection ports
- Parent or group relationship
- Optional semantic type
- Creation and modification timestamps

Supported element categories:

- Shape
- Text
- Connector
- Image
- Icon
- Group
- Container
- Annotation
- Measurement
- Diagram-specific object

## 4.2 Drag-and-drop interactions

Users must be able to drag elements directly from libraries onto the canvas.

Required behaviors:

1. Show a visual preview while dragging.
2. Display valid placement targets.
3. Snap to guides when enabled.
4. Highlight potential relationships.
5. Support inserting objects into containers.
6. Provide immediate feedback for invalid placements.
7. Allow escape to cancel the operation.
8. Support touch and stylus interaction where practical.
9. Preserve object configuration when moving between regions.
10. Support multi-item dragging.

Objects must remain responsive during dragging, including when a diagram contains hundreds of elements.

## 4.3 Object selection

Support:

- Single-click selection
- Multiselect with Shift
- Marquee selection
- Keyboard selection
- Select all
- Selection by type
- Selection by layer
- Selection within a container
- Hierarchical selection for grouped elements

Selected objects must have clear, subtle selection outlines and appropriate handles.

## 4.4 Object manipulation

Users can:

- Move
- Resize
- Rotate
- Duplicate
- Copy
- Paste
- Cut
- Delete
- Group
- Ungroup
- Lock
- Hide
- Bring forward
- Send backward
- Align
- Distribute
- Replace shape
- Change styling

All operations must be reversible through undo.

Dragging a shape should not inadvertently change attached relationships or invalidate its data.

## 4.5 Smart connectors

Connectors are first-class diagram objects, not merely rendered lines.

Supported styles:

- Straight
- Orthogonal
- Curved
- Bezier
- Elbow
- Bidirectional
- Dashed
- Dotted
- Directional
- Labeled

Connectors must support:

- Dynamic attachment points
- Automatic routing
- Obstacle avoidance
- Automatic rerouting when nodes move
- Manual waypoints
- Adjustable endpoints
- Inline labels
- Multiple labels
- Arrowhead selection
- Line thickness and color
- Semantic relationship types

Users should be able to create a connector by dragging from a visible connection handle to another object.

Connection handles appear on hover or selection, rather than cluttering the canvas permanently.

## 4.6 Smart alignment and placement

Provide:

- Alignment guides
- Equal spacing indicators
- Edge snapping
- Center snapping
- Object snapping
- Grid snapping
- Automatic distribution
- Layout suggestions
- Overlap warnings
- Collision-aware placement
- Optional automatic layout

Smart placement must be optional and reversible.

The system should never unexpectedly reorganize an entire diagram during a simple drag operation.

## 4.7 Auto-layout engine

Support layout algorithms for:

- Left-to-right flow
- Top-to-bottom flow
- Hierarchical trees
- Directed graphs
- Swimlanes
- Radial layouts
- Grid arrangements
- Force-directed networks

Users must be able to select an entire diagram or a subset of objects and apply a layout.

Manual positions can be pinned.

Layout operations must preserve semantic relationships and labels.

## 4.8 Canvas navigation

Required behaviors:

- Mouse wheel zoom
- Trackpad pinch zoom
- Touch pinch zoom
- Spacebar plus drag to pan
- Middle-mouse panning
- Zoom around pointer
- Fit selection
- Fit diagram
- Reset viewport
- Mini-map navigation
- Smooth animated viewport transitions

The canvas should support very large diagrams without exhausting browser memory.

## 4.9 Editing history

A comprehensive command-based history system must support undo and redo for:

- Object changes
- Connector changes
- Layout changes
- Text edits
- Group operations
- Style modifications
- Import operations
- Floor-plan geometry edits
- Sequence diagram modifications

Undo should restore meaningful user actions rather than arbitrary low-level state updates.

A single drag gesture should produce one undoable command.

