# 13. AI-assisted diagram generation

## 13.1 Overview

AI capabilities should enhance the visual editing experience rather than become a dependency.

The application must remain fully usable without AI.

AI integration will be optional, modular, and configurable.

## 13.2 Natural language generation

Users can describe a diagram in plain English.

Example:

"Create an architecture showing a React application hosted on Azure communicating with an API gateway, three backend services, Redis cache, and SQL database."

The system should generate an editable diagram.

AI-generated objects must use the same native data structures as manually created objects.

The result cannot be a flattened image.

## 13.3 Natural language sequence creation

Example:

"Show a user logging in through a React frontend, calling an authentication API, validating credentials against an identity provider, then fetching permissions from a database."

The system should create:

- Participants
- Lifelines
- Ordered messages
- Return messages
- Activation bars where appropriate

Users must then be able to modify every element using drag-and-drop.

## 13.4 AI diagram modification

Examples:

- "Add a caching layer."
- "Move the authentication flow before the database call."
- "Group these services under an Azure environment."
- "Make the flowchart more compact."
- "Add an error handling branch."
- "Create a parallel execution fragment."

AI should propose structured changes and display a preview of the affected diagram.

Users can accept, reject, or undo those changes.

## 13.5 Diagram analysis

Potential AI functions:

- Explain architecture
- Summarize workflow
- Identify missing steps
- Suggest simplifications
- Identify apparent circular dependencies
- Highlight ambiguous relationships
- Draft documentation
- Generate a sequence diagram from architecture relationships

AI-generated technical interpretations must be labeled as suggestions rather than verified system behavior.

## 13.6 AI provider architecture

AI must use a provider abstraction.

Potential integrations:

- OpenAI
- Azure OpenAI
- Other compatible model providers
- Enterprise-hosted models

API credentials must remain server-side.

User diagram content must not be sent to an AI provider without an authorized user action or configured organizational policy.

