## 17.1 Architectural direction

The platform should use a modular React-based frontend architecture.

The canvas infrastructure will be shared while diagram-specific editing experiences are implemented as independently maintainable modules.

A recommended structure:

```text
Project Canvas
│
├── Application Shell
│   ├── Navigation
│   ├── Workspace Management
│   ├── Authentication
│   ├── Theme and Design System
│   └── Global Commands
│
├── Core Diagram Engine
│   ├── Document Model
│   ├── Geometry Engine
│   ├── Rendering Engine
│   ├── Selection Engine
│   ├── Drag and Drop
│   ├── Connection Engine
│   ├── Layout Engine
│   ├── Command History
│   └── Serialization
│
├── Diagram Modules
│   ├── Flowchart
│   ├── Architecture
│   ├── Sequence
│   ├── Floor Plan
│   ├── Network
│   ├── ERD
│   ├── Organization Chart
│   └── Whiteboard
│
├── Shared Services
│   ├── Templates
│   ├── Component Libraries
│   ├── Document Storage
│   ├── Search
│   ├── Import / Export
│   ├── Collaboration
│   └── AI Integration
│
└── Backend Services
    ├── API
    ├── Identity and Access
    ├── Document Persistence
    ├── Asset Storage
    ├── Collaboration
    └── AI Gateway
```

## 17.2 Frontend technology

Recommended foundation:

- React
- TypeScript
- Vite
- Modular application shell
- Component-based design system
- Functional programming patterns
- Immutable document updates
- Explicit state management

All diagram modules should expose documented lifecycle and integration contracts.

## 17.3 Canvas rendering strategy

The core engine must separate the semantic document model from visual rendering.

Rendering technologies should be evaluated against requirements such as:

- Connection precision
- Complex text layout
- Scalable geometry
- Large-diagram performance
- Hit testing
- Accessibility
- Export fidelity
- Rich styling

SVG is a strong initial candidate for standard diagram rendering.

Canvas or WebGL rendering may be introduced for very large scenes or specialized workloads.

Hybrid rendering should be supported architecturally.

The product should not assume that one rendering technology is optimal for every diagram type.

## 17.4 Module registration

Diagram modules must register capabilities through a standard interface.

Conceptual TypeScript contract:

```typescript
interface DiagramModule {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  version: string;

  supportedObjects: string[];

  createDocument(): DiagramDocument;

  getToolDefinitions(): ToolDefinition[];

  getPropertyPanels(): PropertyPanelDefinition[];

  getTemplates(): TemplateDefinition[];

  validate(
    document: DiagramDocument
  ): ValidationResult[];

  serialize(
    document: DiagramDocument
  ): unknown;

  deserialize(
    data: unknown
  ): DiagramDocument;
}
```

Modules should not directly manipulate internal state owned by unrelated modules.

## 17.5 Module isolation

Each module should own:

- Specialized object definitions
- Diagram validation
- Layout rules
- Tooling
- Property editors
- Templates
- Module documentation
- Module tests

Shared functionality should be exposed through stable core APIs.

A module failure should not corrupt the global document model.

## 17.6 State management

Separate:

**Document state**

Persistent objects, relationships, styles, and metadata.

**Interaction state**

Selections, active tools, drag operations, hover states, and temporary previews.

**Viewport state**

Zoom, pan, visible region, and presentation settings.

**Application state**

Authentication, workspace configuration, panel visibility, user preferences.

Document changes should use immutable updates and explicit commands.

## 17.7 Command architecture

Represent diagram modifications through commands.

Examples:

- AddObject
- RemoveObject
- MoveObject
- ResizeObject
- AddConnector
- UpdateConnector
- ReorderParticipant
- InsertSequenceMessage
- AddWall
- UpdateRoom

Commands should support:

- Execute
- Undo
- Redo
- Validation
- Transaction grouping
- Serialization where needed

This supports consistent history, testing, and future collaborative editing.

## 17.8 Routing and layout services

The connection-routing engine and automatic-layout engine should be independently testable services.

They should accept structured inputs and return deterministic outputs for equivalent inputs and configuration.

## 17.9 Worker-based processing

Computationally expensive operations should be able to run outside the main UI thread.

Examples:

- Auto-layout
- Connector routing
- Document validation
- Large import parsing
- Complex geometry
- Diagram export

User interactions must remain responsive during these operations.

