# 18. Data architecture and persistence

## 18.1 Local-first development

The application must support fully functional local development without requiring a deployed backend.

Use a persistence abstraction with at least two implementations:

1. IndexedDB-backed local storage
2. Cloud-backed storage

All document operations must go through the same service interface.

## 18.2 Repository interface

Conceptual interface:

```typescript
interface DiagramRepository {
  list(): Promise<DiagramSummary[]>;

  get(id: string): Promise<DiagramDocument>;

  create(
    input: CreateDiagramInput
  ): Promise<DiagramDocument>;

  update(
    id: string,
    changes: DiagramChanges
  ): Promise<DiagramDocument>;

  delete(id: string): Promise<void>;
}
```

Switching storage providers must not require changing diagram components.

## 18.3 Persistence requirements

Support:

- Autosave
- Manual save
- Save recovery
- Schema versioning
- Document validation
- Optimistic updates
- Error recovery
- Migration
- Offline storage

## 18.4 Document schema

Illustrative model:

```typescript
interface DiagramDocument {
  id: string;
  schemaVersion: number;
  type: DiagramType;
  title: string;

  objects: DiagramObject[];
  connections: DiagramConnection[];

  layers: DiagramLayer[];
  groups: DiagramGroup[];

  settings: DiagramSettings;
  metadata: Record<string, unknown>;

  createdAt: string;
  updatedAt: string;
}
```

Specialized diagram modules extend this model through namespaced metadata or explicitly versioned semantic structures.

## 18.5 Stable identifiers

All objects must have durable IDs.

Relationship references must use IDs rather than array positions or screen coordinates.

This is especially important for sequence diagrams, where changing participant order must not change message meaning.

## 18.6 Schema migrations

Every persisted document must declare its schema version.

Opening an older document should invoke necessary migrations before editing.

Migration failures must preserve the original file and present a recovery or export option.

## 18.7 Cloud storage

A future backend may use:

- Relational database for workspaces, permissions, metadata, and version information
- Document storage for diagrams
- Object storage for assets and exports
- Real-time synchronization service for collaboration

The cloud implementation must preserve compatibility with the native document schema.

